L632: 2
L633: **1: Clinical outcome protocols**
L634: 1.1 Incremental Cardiopulmonary Exercise test (CPET)
L635: Incremental cardiopulmonary exercise test (CPET) followed protocol and guidelines of the perioperative exercise testing and
L636: training society (POETs) (1) and the American Thoracic Society/American College of Chest Physicians.(2) Identical protocol was
L637: employed for both IPF and Control participants. Safety criteria for the test were employed as documented in these guidelines.
L638: For each patient the estimated ramp slope was first calculated using established formula derived by Wasserman et al. (3) then
L639: adjusted pragmatically based on the patients physiological status to obtain a test time of approximately 8-12 minutes. During
L640: incremental CPET active encouragement was given to patients to facilitate best individual performance in the test. The test
L641: was terminated either by the patient indicating that they had reached their symptom limitation or if their pedalling cadence
L642: fell to less than 50 rpm for greater than 10 s. For subsequent CPETs the same ramp slope as baseline was employed. V̇O2peak
L643: percent predicted values were calculated from equations derived by Hansen and Wasserman.(3) All CPETs were conducted in
L644: the morning or early afternoon (0800-1300) and patients were instructed to attend the research centre fasted and provided
L645: with a light breakfast of toast, butter, spread, jam or cheese at least 2 hours prior to the test. This dietary restriction was
L646: necessary to control for nitrogen containing foods which would influence blood analysis of redox markers. One participant was
L647: taking the antioxidant N-acetyl cysteine (NAC) at a dose of 600 mg three times a day.
L648: 1.2 Constant work-rate exercise test (CWRT)
L649: The CWRT was performed at a separate visit at least 48 hours after incremental CPET ( **Supplemental Figure 1** ). The CWRT was
L650: performed with near identical protocol to CPET. However instead of the incremental ramp phase following unloaded pedalling
L651: the work-rate of the test (75% of work rate achieved at V̇O2peak during baseline incremental CPET) was introduced ‘on-block’.
L652: After baseline CWRT patients were not informed of their achieved Endurance time to help prevent bias at the final assessment
L653: of ‘having a target’ to achieve. The test was terminated either by the patient indicating that they had reached their symptom
L654: limitation or if pedalling cadence fell to less than 50 rpm for greater than 10 s.
L655: 1.3 Spirometry and Gas transfer assessment
L656: All spirometry was reported to reference ranges of the Global Lung Initiative (GLI) 2012(4) and technical specifications of the
L657: European Respiratory Society (ERS) and ATS.(5) Single-breath carbon monoxide uptake in the lung testing is reported to
L658: technical standards of the ERS and ATS 2017(6).
L659: 1.4 Medical Research breathlessness scale
L660: The Medical Research Council breathlessness score was completed as a patient reported outcome of symptomatic
L661: breathlessness. The Medical Research Council Breathlessness score adapted from (7):
L662: |Grade|Degree of breathlessness related to activities|
L663: |---|---|
L664: |**1 **|Not troubled by breathlessness except on strenuous exercise|
L665: |**2 **|Short of breath when hurrying on the level or walking up a slight hill|
L666: |**3 **|Walks slower than most people on the level, stops after a mile or so, or stops after 15 minutes walking at own<br>pace|
L667: |**4 **|Stops for breath after walking about 100 yards, or after a few minutes on level ground|
L668: |**5 **|Too breathless to leave the house, or breathless when undressing|
L669: 1.5 St. George’s respiratory questionnaire
L670: The IPF Specific version of the St. Georges respiratory questionnaire was used as a validated patient reported health related
L671: quality of life assessment tool.(8)
L672: 3
L673: **2: Structured Responsive Exercise Training Programme**
L674: **2.1** **Description of the SRETP following the Consensus on Exercise Reporting Template (9)**
L675: **Type of exercise equipment used**
L676: Exercise training consisted of 30 min (Week 1) and 40 min (Week 2-8) -including 5 min warm-up and 5 min cool-down- of
L677: interval training on an electromagnetically braked cycle ergometer (Optibike Ergoline GmbH, Germany). The training
L678: programme was preloaded on a chip-and-pin card which executed the interval intensities automatically.
L679: **Qualifications, teaching/supervising expertise, and/or training undertaken by the exercise instructor**
L680: The exercise intervention was supervised by hospital staff with a minimal qualification of Basic Life Support. These included
L681: physiotherapists, sports medicine practitioners, research nurses and study doctors. Each participant’s first exercise session was
L682: supervised by a medically trained clinician. A named medically trained clinician was available on-site during training sessions.
L683: **Describe whether the exercises are performed individually or in a group**
L684: Wherever possible, dependent on availability, trial participants were exercised in pairs, to provide camaraderie.
L685: **Exercise supervision and delivery**
L686: Prior to each exercise session participants were asked if they felt well enough to perform the exercise session, asked about
L687: possible intercurrent illness, and had focused physical examination and vital signs recorded. During the session the participants
L688: were reminded to inform the supervisor of chest pain, sudden shortness of breath and dizziness as the POETs guidelines. During
L689: exercise training session continuous heart rate was monitored by R-R interval and peripheral oxygen saturation recorded. The
L690: study protocol stated that if oxygen saturations dropped to <85% during sessions participants were required to rest until
L691: recovery to baseline levels however no participants met this threshold during training sessions.
L692: **How fidelity/ adherence to exercise was measured and reported**
L693: Adherence was reported as the number of exercise sessions completed divided by the total number of sessions available
L694: (n=16). In the study protocol a minimum of 12 out of 16 sessions (≥75%) was pre-specified as the minimum adherence required
L695: to complete the programme.
L696: **Description of motivation strategies**
L697: All exercise sessions were supervised throughout as described above. No specific motivational strategies were adopted
L698: although participants were given gentle positive encouragement through the exercise sessions.
L699: **Exercise progression**
L700: Exercise intensity is prescribed onto a chip and pin card, using the participant’s physiological variables determined by CPET. To
L701: ensure that progressive overload was maintained throughout the trial, participants underwent CPET after 4 weeks and the
L702: training programme was modified for each individual's ramped CPET protocol results ensuring consistent, progressive and
L703: individualized intensities for all subjects.
L704: 4
L705: **Detailed description of the exercise training sessions**
L706: Exercise training consisted of 30 min (Week 1) and 40 min (Week 2-8) -including 5 min warm-up and 5 min cool-down- of
L707: interval, aerobic training on an electromagnetically braked cycle ergometer (Optibike Ergoline GmbH, Germany). All IPF
L708: participants were invited to attend an outpatient based, in-hospital, 8 week, twice weekly structured, responsive exercise
L709: training programme. Participants wore a heartrate monitor throughout the session which reported pulse rate onto the
L710: ergometer screen. Training intensities were prescribed onto a chip and pin card, based on individual’s physiological variables,
L711: (anaerobic threshold (AT) and V̇O2peak) determined at CPET. This card was inserted into the cycle ergometer and participants
L712: instructed to pedal at a cadence of 60-65 revolution per minute, according to a visible reading. Sessions lasted for 30 min (Week
L713: 1) or 40 min (Week 2-8), starting with a 5-minute warm-up at 0-5 watts. After 5 minutes warm-up, the interval components
L714: began with 3 minutes, moderate intensity, at a work rate equal to 80% of that achieved at anaerobic threshold (AT). This was
L715: followed by high intensity exercise, for 2 minutes at a work rate equal to 50% of the difference in work rate between AT and
L716: V̇O2peak (for graphical representation of training session see **Supplementary Section S2.2** ). This 5-minute interval was then
L717: repeated; 4 times (Week 1) or 6 times (Week 2-8), followed by a 5-minute recovery period at 0-5 watts. In total, exercise
L718: sessions lasted for 30-40 minutes as described. Progress through the session, pedalling cadence, and heart rate were visible to
L719: the participant on a digital display at the head of the cycle ergometer ( **see Supplementary Section 2.3** ) **.**
L720: **Home programme/non-exercise component**
L721: All study exercise participation took place in-hospital as an out-patient. No home exercises were prescribed, and participants
L722: were instructed to continue with only their usual daily activities in addition to the exercise programme for the duration of the
L723: study. No non-exercise components were included in this study.
L724: **Type and number of adverse events**
L725: Exercise related adverse events would be deemed attributable if occurring within 30 minutes of exercise or CPET. No serious
L726: adverse events were reported. 2 mild (Grade 1) adverse events were reported; 1 after CPET and 1 after an exercise training
L727: session. Both mild post-exercise presyncope which resolved after oral hydration and a brief period of observation.
L728: **Setting in which the exercise was performed**
L729: Exercise sessions were performed in an outpatient, in-hospital setting, within a designated research physiology laboratory.
L730: **Extent of adherence**
L731: During the study the median adherence of the 11 participants who completed the SRETP was 88% (min. 75% - max. 94%). All
L732: 11 participants achieved ≥75% adherence. 2 participants were within the exercise training programme at point of study
L733: termination due to the COVID-19 pandemic. The individual adherence for each of these individuals (factored for the number
L734: of weeks training completed) was 75% (9/12 sessions) and 100% (4/4 sessions).
L735: 5
